<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
	Settings schema, format version 1.

	Every id here is the id the old flat schema used. That is the whole
	migration story: Kodi keys stored values by id in
	addon_data/script.embuary.info/settings.xml, so an id that survives keeps
	its value and an id that is renamed silently resets the user to default.
	Verified on Kodi 21.3 against a value file holding non-default values for
	all eleven pre-existing settings; see docs/measurements.md.

	Changing a <default> only reaches installs that have never stored the
	setting. Verified on Kodi 21.3: filter_rating's default moved from 0 to
	4.0, and an install already holding
	<setting id="filter_rating" default="true">0</setting> still read 0 after a
	restart, keeping both the value and the now-wrong default="true" flag.
	filter_votes, which had no row at all, took its new default of 5. So a
	stored row wins and the flag is carried over from the file rather than
	recomputed against the schema, which means anyone who has run the add-on
	before keeps what they had until they press Defaults.

	Labels that had a description crammed into them have been shortened and
	the description moved to a help string. Where that changed the English
	text, a NEW string id was allocated rather than editing the existing
	msgid: 70-odd strings.po files carry translations of the current text,
	and rewriting an msgid in place leaves every one of them silently wrong.

	No group carries a label. Kodi draws a group label as a heading inside the
	category, and with three short categories those headings were an extra
	level of hierarchy over lists of four or five rows. Each category is one
	flat list instead, which is also why every setting in a category now needs
	a label that reads on its own. The three API keys were all called "API
	key"; only the headings told them apart.
-->
